  "summary_text": "Kyle, Port Washington is firing on all cylinders and Hicksville is sending a warning signal\u2014not in yesterday's numbers, which look strong, but in the pattern underneath.\n\nPort Washington delivered $3,420 yesterday, up 4% on transaction-flat traffic. The real story: grooming revenue jumped 15%, core grooms up 12% week-over-week, and add-on attach climbed from 59% to 80%. That's discipline. You're running 5 groomers at $474/day against a 30-day baseline of $516\u2014a 8% lag, but yesterday you had bather support at 7 hours, above your 4.5h daily average. The retail dip to $430 is noise; grooming is the engine and it's humming. MTD you're tracking dead-even to DOW-adjusted expectation (+6% actual vs +6% calendar-adjusted), and the 12+ week visit-cadence bucket is shrinking, which means you're pulling customers back in. Year-to-date is +49% revenue on +50% appointments\u2014that's proportional scaling.\n\nHicksville's yesterday ($1,523, +14%) masks a softer week-to-date (-21% YoY) and a worrying cadence signal: the 12+ week bucket is growing 2.4 points to 12.6%, a leading indicator of churn. You're DOW-adjusted +17.5% MTD, which is real, but transaction count collapsed 32% yesterday even though revenue held\u2014that's ticket-size inflation masking volume loss. Add-ons improved to 31% attach, but your retail and transaction patterns suggest front-of-house engagement is slipping. Hicksville runs lean at 2.2 groomer-days/day vs Port's 4.5; you need to decide if that's structural or if there's capacity headroom to test.\n\n**Capacity & coverage: Port Washington has 6 groomers and 7.0h bathers scheduled today; Hicksville has 3 groomers (0h bathers); next 7 days across both stores: 47 groomer-days total.**"
